option.FOXY_WEATHER_ENABLED=Weather cycle
value.FOXY_WEATHER_ENABLED.0=Off
value.FOXY_WEATHER_ENABLED.1=On
option.FOXY_WEATHER_ENABLED.comment=Cycles the weather by world day: day 1 uses day 1's setup, day 7 is day 1 again, and so on. The shipped cycle varies (cloudy / clear / few / overcast / gale / fog), with day 1 keeping the old cloudy default; set all six days the same to make it constant.
option.FOXY_WEATHER_TRANSITION=Weather changeover (in-game hours)
option.FOXY_WEATHER_TRANSITION.comment=After each sunrise the weather fades from yesterday's setup to today's over this long. 0 switches instantly at sunrise.
option.FOXY_WEATHER_OFFSET=Today counts as day
option.FOXY_WEATHER_OFFSET.comment=Lines a cycle day up with the world's first day, so the cycle can be matched to the day being played without editing all six pages.
option.FOXY_WEATHER_GALE_LEAN=Gale lean
option.FOXY_WEATHER_GALE_LEAN.comment=How far a gale bends grass, fern and flowers over along the wind, in blocks at the tip. The swinging on top of it is the pack's own gust, so together they read as blown over and shaking; leaves are cubes and are left out of the lean.
screen.FOXY_WEATHER_PARTICLES_OPTIONS=Rain & Snow
screen.FOXY_WEATHER_SLANT_OPTIONS=Wind lean
option.FOXY_WEATHER_OPACITY=Precipitation opacity
option.FOXY_WEATHER_OPACITY.comment=Light returned per rain or snow particle. These particles are long vertical billboards, so the projection stretches or crushes them by a large factor; this is the base value, and the final opacity is normalized by how much screen the particle actually covers. Raise for heavier rain, lower for a lighter veil.
option.FOXY_WEATHER_NEAR_FADE=Near fade distance
option.FOXY_WEATHER_NEAR_FADE.comment=Particles closer than this to the eye smear across the screen and turn the layer into a solid curtain, so they fade out smoothly over this range. Smaller keeps the fade tighter and stops the layer saturating when looking down, but too small leaves the rain thin. 0 disables the fade.
option.FOXY_WEATHER_SPLASH_OPACITY=Splash opacity
option.FOXY_WEATHER_SPLASH_OPACITY=Splash opacity
option.FOXY_WEATHER_SPLASH_OPACITY.comment=Opacity of the little burst vanilla raises where rain lands. That texture is solid where the falling streak is mostly empty, so it needs its own weight and the near fade does not apply to it. Lower for lighter bursts, raise for a more emphatic impact.
option.FOXY_WEATHER_SCATTER_FLOOR=Scattering floor
option.FOXY_WEATHER_SCATTER_FLOOR.comment=Drops and flakes scatter light strongly forward, which is why real streaks stay readable against a dark sky instead of being crushed into the background. This puts a floor under the particle luminance: it lifts energy and keeps the hue, never shifts the colour. Raise for more presence in the dark, 0 to follow the sky exactly.
option.FOXY_RAIN_DIRECT_LIGHT_FLOOR=Rain sunlight retention
option.FOXY_RAIN_DIRECT_LIGHT_FLOOR.comment=How much of the global direct sunlight survives full rain, which is what sells a sunshower. Cloud shadows still decide per pixel where that sunlight lands; this only sets how much sun there is overall. 1.0 removes the global dimming entirely and lets cloud shadows carry the weather alone.
option.FOXY_WEATHER_SLANT=Wind lean amount
option.FOXY_WEATHER_SLANT.comment=How far rain and snow drift sideways per block of fall. The lean is applied in world space, so it belongs to the particle rather than to the camera.
option.FOXY_WEATHER_SLANT_WAVE=Lean variation
option.FOXY_WEATHER_SLANT_WAVE.comment=Gives each streak its own lean angle so the rain does not read as a set of parallel lines. 0 makes every streak lean by the same amount.
option.FOXY_WEATHER_DAY1=Day 1 weather
option.FOXY_WEATHER_DAY1.comment=The world's first day. Clear / Few clouds / Cloudy / Overcast / Fog / Gale. Cloudy matches the old default; fog does not thin out around noon; overcast leaves a fifth of the direct sunlight; the gale is that fog with a wind - few-clouds coverage, clouds four times as fast, the ground aerosol twelve times, and grass bent over downwind and swinging harder. Overcast and fog are capped at 0.65 coverage - past that the cloud field saturates and its shapes distort.
